.TH EUCLID-CLI-EAG 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-eag \- publish paths through the API gateway onto euclid applications
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli
[\fB\-\-endpoint\fR \fIURL\fR]
[\fB\-\-loglevel\fR \fILEVEL\fR]
.B eag
.I action
[\fIaction-options\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
The
.B eag
module configures euclid's API gateway: an HTTP listener of its own that
publishes paths to the outside world and proxies each request to one of
the running instances of a
.BR euclid-cli-eap (1)
application.
.PP
It exists because an autoscaled application has no fixed address. Eight
instances of a parser are eight processes on eight ports, and those
ports change as the autoscaler starts and stops them \- so nothing
outside can be told where to send a request. The gateway is the fixed
address: one port that does not move, in front of a set of backends that
does.
.PP
This is not the same listener as
.I euclid.gateway.http.port
(5566), which carries euclid's own service calls \- the ones every other
module in this manual is reached through. That one speaks euclid's
protocol and routes by
.IR x-euclid-target .
The API gateway speaks nothing but HTTP and routes by path, because what
is behind it is an ordinary web application that knows nothing about
euclid.
.SH ROUTES
A route is a path prefix plus the application it belongs to. Everything
beneath the prefix is carried, and the path is forwarded
.I unchanged
\- a route on
.I /resource
pointing at
.I parser
sends
.PP
.RS
.nf
GET /resource/searchById?id=123
.fi
.RE
.PP
to the parser exactly as written. The application sees the URL its
callers typed; nothing about the route, the gateway or the application's
own name appears in it. An application therefore does not have to know
it is behind a gateway, and moving a path from one application to
another changes no URL.
.PP
Longest prefix wins. A route on
.I /resource
carries everything below it, and a more specific
.I /resource/export
added later takes precedence for its own subtree without either being
reordered \- the more specific one is simply longer.
.PP
A prefix only matches on a segment boundary, so
.I /resource
never claims
.IR /resource\-intern ,
which is a different resource that happens to start with the same
letters.
.PP
.SH METHODS
A route may name the HTTP methods it answers for. Naming none \- the
default \- means all of them, so a route written today does not quietly
refuse a method that did not exist when it was written.
.PP
Two routes may share a path as long as their methods do not overlap.
That is what lets one resource be served by two applications, reads by
one and writes by another, with no seam a caller can see:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route -r produkte-lesen  -p /api/produkte \e
    -a parser --methods GET,HEAD
euclid-cli eag create-route -r produkte-pflege -p /api/produkte \e
    -a pim-writer --methods POST,PUT,DELETE
.fi
.RE
.PP
An overlap is refused, because the winner would be decided by whichever
route the gateway happened to consider first \- callers would reach one
application today and possibly the other tomorrow. A route naming no
methods overlaps with everything, so it cannot share a path at all.
.PP
Matching takes the method into account rather than filtering on it
afterwards: a
.B GET
that a POST-only route would reject falls through to the route that does
want it. Only when no route on the path accepts the method is the
request refused, with
.B 405
and an
.I Allow
header listing what the routes on that path do accept.
.SH BACKENDS
The instances behind a route are not configured. The manager gives every
application instance a TCP port of its own out of the range set by
.I euclid.modules.eap.http\-port\-min
and
.I euclid.modules.eap.http\-port\-max
(9000\-9999 by default) and records it, and the gateway reads that record
\- so an application that scales from one instance to eight is spread
across eight backends within a few seconds, and one that scales back
down stops being sent to, without any route being touched.
.PP
Requests go to the instances in turn, one after another. An instance the
manager could not give a port to is left out of the rotation rather than
being sent to and failing.
.PP
An application must set its own listener to the port it is given. For
Spring Boot that is
.PP
.RS
.nf
server:
  port: ${EUCLID_HTTP_PORT:8080}
.fi
.RE
.PP
which is what makes several instances able to run on one host at all:
without it every instance binds the same port, the first one wins, and
the rest fail in a way the autoscaler cannot see.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Each route decides what it requires of a caller, because the two kinds of
resource sit behind the same listener.
.TP
.B NONE
Proxied as it arrives. Whatever the application requires, it enforces
itself. This is what a route behind an external API gateway wants, and
what a public read wants.
.TP
.B EUCLID
A euclid credential is required and verified before anything is
forwarded \- whatever the euclid gateway accepts, which today is a
bearer token, an RFC 9421 message signature or SigV4. The check happens
before a backend is chosen, so an unauthenticated caller never causes a
connection to an application at all.
.SH ANSWERS
Five of the gateway's own answers are worth telling apart, since none of
them come from the application:
.TP
.B 404
No route claims this path, or the route that did has been deleted or
deactivated.
.TP
.B 405
A route claims the path but none on it accepts this method. The
.I Allow
header says which methods would have worked.
.TP
.B 401
The route requires a euclid credential and the request had none, or one
that did not verify.
.TP
.B 503
The route exists and the application has no running instance \- scaled
to zero, still starting, or given no port. The resource exists and
trying again is reasonable, which is why this is not a 404.
.TP
.B 502
An instance was chosen and did not answer: the connection was refused,
or it took longer than 60 seconds.
.SH CHANGES
The gateway re-reads its routes every
.I euclid.modules.eag.refresh\-seconds
(5 by default), so a route created, changed or deleted here takes effect
shortly after the command returns rather than at the next restart. A
database it cannot read leaves the previous routes in place, so a
momentary outage there does not become an outage of every application
behind the gateway.
.SH ACTIONS
.TP
.B create-route
Publishes a path prefix and points it at an application. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-create-route (1).
.TP
.B delete-route
Removes a route, taking its path out of service. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-delete-route (1).
.TP
.B get-route
Shows one route's definition. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-get-route (1).
.TP
.B list-routes
Lists the configured routes. See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-list-routes (1).
.TP
.B update-route
Changes an existing route's path, application, authentication or state.
See
.BR euclid-cli-eag-update-route (1).
.SH GLOBAL OPTIONS
These options are given to
.B euclid-cli
itself, before the
.I eag
module name.
.TP
.BR \-e ", " \-\-endpoint " " \fIURL\fR
Euclid gateway endpoint to talk to, e.g. \fIhttps://localhost:5566\fR.
Note that this is euclid's own service endpoint, not the API gateway
port these commands configure.
.TP
.BR \-\-ca\-cert " " \fIPATH\fR
Path to a PEM CA certificate to trust in addition to the system trust
store, e.g. for self-signed development certificates.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-pretty " " \fIBOOL\fR
Pretty-print the JSON response. Defaults to \fItrue\fR.
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signature " " \fISCHEME\fR
Signature scheme used for signed service calls: \fIrfc9421\fR (the
default) or \fIsigv4\fR for AWS Signature Version 4.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-loglevel " " \fILEVEL\fR
Log level: one of \fItrace\fR, \fIdebug\fR, \fIinfo\fR, \fIwarning\fR,
\fIerror\fR, \fIfatal\fR. Defaults to \fIinfo\fR.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print top-level usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-version
Print the euclid-cli version and exit.
.SH AUTHENTICATION
Every
.B eag
action is administrator-only, as in
.BR euclid-cli-ets (1):
a route decides what the installation exposes to the outside world and
whether reaching it needs a credential at all.
.PP
The session is created by
.BR euclid-cli-eam-login (1)
and loaded from
.IR $HOME/.euclid/credentials .
The CLI checks for it before sending, but that is only a fail-fast for a
clearer message \- the server re-checks every request regardless.
.PP
Requesting help for an action does not require a session, so
.B "euclid-cli eag <action> \-\-help"
works before logging in.
.SH CONFIGURATION
Read from
.I euclid.modules.eag
in
.IR euclid.json :
.TP
.I port
TCP port the API gateway listens on. Defaults to 8080.
.TP
.I proxy\-threads
Threads serving proxied requests. Defaults to 8.
.TP
.I refresh\-seconds
How often routes and backends are re-read from the database. Defaults
to 5.
.TP
.I maxInstances
Must be 1. Two instances cannot bind the same port, and the autoscaler
cannot see that failure \- it would only see a pool that will not grow.
.SH LIMITATIONS
Request and response bodies are buffered whole rather than streamed,
which suits the short REST calls the gateway carries today and not a
large download. One request is served per connection; there is no
keep-alive to the caller and no connection pooling to the backends.
.PP
A backend that refuses the connection produces a 502 rather than a retry
against another instance of the same application.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The action completed successfully.
.TP
.B 1
Argument parsing failed, there is no session or it is not an
administrator's, the request could not be sent, or the server rejected
it. Details are printed to standard error.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I $HOME/.euclid/credentials
JSON file holding the current session's bearer token and/or access key,
created by
.B euclid-cli eam login
and read by every
.B eag
action.
.SH EXAMPLES
Publish a parser's REST interface, requiring no credential because an
external gateway in front of euclid authenticates the caller:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route \e
    --route-id resource \e
    --path /resource \e
    --application onix-parser

curl http://localhost:8080/resource/searchById?id=123
.fi
.RE
.PP
Carve out an administrative subtree of the same application that does
require one:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag create-route \e
    --route-id resource-admin \e
    --path /resource/admin \e
    --application onix-parser \e
    --authentication euclid
.fi
.RE
.PP
Take a path out of service without losing how it was configured, then
put it back:
.PP
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli eag update-route --route-id resource --active false
euclid-cli eag update-route --route-id resource --active true
.fi
.RE
